        safetyLevel: "critical",
        body: "**RULE**\n\u2022 Hepatic artery or portal vein stenosis or thrombosis should be managed in a timely fashion, which is particularly urgent when the hepatic artery is involved (Strong, Level 4). [3]\n\u2022 Doppler ultrasound is widely used as a screening tool to assess the liver graft vessels in the immediate postoperative period. [3]\n\u2022 Contrast-enhanced CT angiography or venography gives more detail when a vascular abnormality is suspected or when Doppler findings are inconclusive; MRI can be used as an alternative when contrast-enhanced CT is contraindicated. [3]\n\u2022 When early hepatic artery thrombosis (within 5 days of transplant) leads to progressive liver failure, urgent re-transplantation is needed. [3]\n\u2022 For stenosis or thrombosis: surgical or percutaneous endovascular intervention and anticoagulation should be considered for cases detected in the immediate postoperative period, and percutaneous endovascular intervention and/or anticoagulation for diagnoses made later (Strong, Level 4); the choice of intervention is mainly driven by center practice and the condition of the patient. [3]\n\u2022 Editor note: name suspected vascular compromise to the transplant team at the first call.\nRelated consults: [GI bleed hub](#/tree/gi-bleed-hub) (variceal bleeding); [Paracentesis](#/tree/paracentesis) (ascites).\nDetail: [vascular detail](#/info/tpe-liver-vascular-detail).",
