        body: "\nKDIGO 2009, kidney recipients:\n\u2022 allopurinol with azathioprine: use with extreme caution or not at all; if used together, reduce azathioprine by at least 50% and monitor counts (rationale) (call the transplant team first) [10]\n\u2022 Imuran label, if used together: about 1/3 to 1/4 of the usual azathioprine dose (call the transplant team first); febuxostat: Uloric label, contraindicated with azathioprine [18, 19]\n\u2022 NSAIDs and COX-2 inhibitors can significantly reduce kidney function and cause acute kidney injury (rationale) [10]\n\u2022 suggest colchicine for acute gout, with dose reduction for reduced kidney function and concomitant CNI use (2D) [10]\n\u2022 colchicine levels may be raised in reduced kidney function and with cyclosporine, and presumably tacrolimus (rationale) [10]\n\u2022 dose: in patients with eGFR under 60, avoid prolonged colchicine and avoid doses above 0.6 mg/day (rationale) [10]\n\u2022 rifampin strongly induces enzymes that metabolize CNIs and mTOR inhibitors, so adequate levels may be hard to keep (rationale) [10]\n\u2022 Not in KDIGO 2009: cautions for macrolides, aminoglycosides or vancomycin. Nearest: the guideline notes interactions between fluconazole and CNIs, and a report of a possible clotrimazole-tacrolimus interaction (rationale) [10]\n[Details](#/info/tpe-kidney-drugs-detail); [Tacrolimus and cyclosporine labels](#/tree/transplant-patient-ed/node/tpe-cni-drugs)\nRelated consults: [Sepsis](#/tree/sepsis/node/sepsis-start) (fever, shock).",
