        summary: "CMV consensus 2025: send CMV PCR on blood; blood DNA can be low or undetectable in gut disease and, rarely, in lung recipients with pneumonia.",
        body: "**CMV RISK**\n\u2022 High risk is donor CMV+/recipient CMV\u2212 (D+/R\u2212); intermediate D+/R+ or D\u2212/R+; low D\u2212/R\u2212. [22]\n\u2022 Late-onset CMV (after prophylaxis ends) is associated with D+/R\u2212, shorter prophylaxis, higher immunosuppression and rejection; observational studies show more CMV after rejection treatment with lymphocyte-depleting antibodies. [22]\n\u2022 **Blood CMV DNA can be low or undetectable in gastrointestinal disease and, rarely, in lung recipients with CMV pneumonia.** [22]\n\u2022 Serology has no role in diagnosing active CMV. [22]\n\u2022 Liver recipients: CMV can present as asymptomatic viremia, CMV syndrome (fatigue, myalgias, fevers, neutropenia, viremia) or end-organ disease, most often gastrointestinal, including the liver. [23]\n\n[Timing, presentation and related consults](#/info/tpe-cmv-risk)\n\nEditor note: tell the transplant team the organ, transplant date, donor and recipient CMV status, and when prophylaxis ended or rejection was treated.\n\nEditor note: call the transplant team before changing, holding or stopping any immunosuppressant.",
