        title: "Transplant patient on tacrolimus or cyclosporine: possible drug toxicity",
        safetyLevel: "warning",
        summary: "Tacrolimus labels: for neurotoxicity, or raised creatinine with a trough above range, consider dosage reduction (call the transplant team first). Not in the sources used: one toxic level.",
        body: "\n\u2022 Tacrolimus labels: with a raised creatinine and a trough \"greater than the recommended range\", \"consider dosage reduction or temporary interruption of tacrolimus administration\"; for neurotoxicity (PRES, delirium, seizure, coma), \"consider dosage reduction or discontinuation\" (call the transplant team first). [27, 28, 29]\n\u2022 Also named: hyperkalemia, hypertension, thrombotic microangiopathy (see the TMA node); diabetes (tacrolimus labels: new-onset diabetes; liver guideline: \"diabetes (more with TAC)\"; Neoral: hyperglycemia in 2% or less); QT prolongation with torsades (tacrolimus labels only); low magnesium (tacrolimus adverse reactions; cyclosporine convulsions). [27, 30, 35]\n\u2022 Not in the labels or guidelines used: one toxic level. The consensus says a single target range cannot be recommended, and the level cautions are organ specific ([target ranges](#/info/tpe-cni-targets)). [33]\n\u2022 The tacrolimus Medication Guides (Prograf, Astagraf XL, Envarsus XR; \"How should I take ...?\") say: \"Do not stop taking or change your dose of [the drug] without talking to your healthcare provider.\" [27, 28, 29]\n\nEditor note: call the transplant team before changing, holding or stopping any immunosuppressant.\n\nEditor note: ask which calcineurin inhibitor product the patient takes and how to reach the transplant centre.",
