        title: "Possible Pneumocystis pneumonia (PJP)",
        safetyLevel: "warning",
        summary: "AST IDCOP 2019: most empiric pneumonia regimens will not optimally treat Pneumocystis. Not in the guidelines read: treatment before diagnosis. KDIGO 2009 (kidney, 1C): PCP diagnosed by lavage or biopsy.",
        body: "\nEditor note: call the transplant team before changing, holding or stopping any immunosuppressant.\n\u2022 Not in the guidelines read: whether to start Pneumocystis treatment before a diagnosis. KDIGO 2009, kidney, addresses PCP already diagnosed by lavage or biopsy and does not address starting earlier. [65]\n\u2022 Nearest sourced fact: AST IDCOP 2019: most empiric pneumonia regimens will not optimally treat Nocardia or Pneumocystis; late-onset and outbreak-associated PJP remain a problem despite prophylaxis (ungraded). [68]\n\u2022 AST IDCOP 2019, same chapter: for the slower-onset pathogens it lists (Nocardia, tuberculosis, non-tuberculous mycobacteria, endemic fungi, invasive molds), empiric therapy is usually not instituted before testing, but may be needed with high acuity or inability to perform procedures; Pneumocystis is not named in that passage. [68]\n\u2022 App content: the HIV consult's pulmonary opportunistic infection node gives Pneumocystis treatment for patients with HIV.\n\u2022 Related consults: [Pneumonia](#/tree/pneumonia/node/pna-start), pneumonia care; [Sepsis](#/tree/sepsis/node/sepsis-start), sepsis care; [HIV pulmonary infections](#/tree/hiv/node/hiv-pulm-oi), Pneumocystis in HIV; [Kidney recipient infections](#/tree/transplant-patient-ed/node/tpe-kidney-infection), kidney page.\n\u2022 More: [Pneumocystis statements and source differences](#/info/tpe-pjp-detail).",
        citation: [65, 68],
