        body: "\nEditor note: call the transplant team before changing, holding or stopping any immunosuppressant.\n\u2022 Not in the guidelines read: an ED red-flag list for PTLD, when to image, or which tests to send. Nearest sourced fact: AASLD/AST 2025, liver: presentation is often nonspecific, with vague symptoms such as fevers, night sweats or weight loss; imaging may show bulky lymphadenopathy; diagnosis is by biopsy. [74]\n\u2022 KDIGO 2009, kidney: EBV disease can present as a nonspecific febrile illness, gastroenteritis or hepatitis, which may look like CMV or other infection (rationale, ungraded). [57]\n\u2022 Review (emergency medicine, 2018; narrative review, framing only): EBV may present initially with fever, malaise, fatigue, weight loss and lymphadenopathy. [40]\n\u2022 App content: the Lymphadenopathy consult is a general chief-complaint hub for swollen nodes.\n\u2022 Related consults: [Lymphadenopathy](#/tree/lymphadenopathy-hub/node/lad-start), swollen nodes; [Oncological Emergencies](#/tree/oncological-emergencies/node/onc-start), cancer emergencies; [Brain symptoms](#/tree/transplant-patient-ed/node/tpe-cni-brain), neurotoxicity; [Sepsis](#/tree/sepsis/node/sepsis-start), sepsis care; [CMV](#/tree/transplant-patient-ed/node/tpe-cmv), CMV page.\n\u2022 More: [PTLD statements and source differences](#/info/tpe-ptld-detail).",
